The journal geometry is checked before it is believed

The journal superblock states its own block size, log length, first log
block and start position, and nothing bounded any of them. Everything
downstream assumes journal blocks and filesystem blocks are the same size:
checksums run `blocksize` bytes over a filesystem-block buffer, replay
memcpys `blocksize` bytes into one, tag tables are walked to `blocksize`.
A corrupt blocksize larger than the real one turned each of those into an
out-of-bounds read -- and the checksum helper into an out-of-bounds WRITE,
since it zeroes the tail checksum in place before computing. That runs on
every descriptor and revoke block of every recovery mount, and on every
commit of every read-write session.

The superblock's own CRC does not defend this: a crafted image checksums
itself consistently, and journals without the checksum feature (anything
mke2fs makes without metadata_csum) carry no CRC at all.

jbd_get_fs() now refuses a journal whose blocksize differs from the
filesystem's, whose maxlen is zero or larger than the journal file itself,
or whose first/start lie beyond maxlen -- one check at the door instead of
faith at every use. Red cells in Tests/run_bounds_tests.sh corrupt each
field on an mke2fs-built volume and require a clean refusal under a
deadline: a crash or a wedge is a failed test.

diff --git a/src/ext4_journal.c b/src/ext4_journal.c
--- a/src/ext4_journal.c
+++ b/src/ext4_journal.c
@@ -43,6 +43,7 @@
 
 #include <ext4_fs.h>
 #include <ext4_super.h>
+#include <ext4_inode.h>
 #include <ext4_journal.h>
 #include <ext4_blockdev.h>
 #include <ext4_crc32.h>
@@ -855,6 +856,37 @@ int jbd_get_fs(struct ext4_fs *fs,
 		goto Error;
 	}
 
+	/* The geometry every user of this superblock assumes, checked once
+	 * where the superblock enters. Everything downstream treats journal
+	 * blocks and filesystem blocks as the same size -- checksums run
+	 * `blocksize` bytes over a filesystem-block buffer, replay memcpys
+	 * `blocksize` bytes into one, tag tables are walked to `blocksize`.
+	 * A corrupt field larger than the real block size turned each of
+	 * those into an out-of-bounds read, and the checksum helper into an
+	 * out-of-bounds WRITE (it zeroes the tail checksum in place) -- on
+	 * every descriptor block of every recovery mount. The self-seeded
+	 * CRC in jbd_verify_sb cannot catch it: a corrupt superblock
+	 * checksums itself consistently. `maxlen` is bounded by the journal
+	 * file's own size for the same reason: `wrap` subtracts it from
+	 * block indexes, and replay trusts the difference. */
+	if (jbd_get32(&jbd_fs->sb, blocksize) !=
+	    ext4_sb_get_block_size(&fs->sb)) {
+		rc = EIO;
+		goto Error;
+	}
+	{
+		uint64_t inode_size = ext4_inode_get_size(&fs->sb,
+					jbd_fs->inode_ref.inode);
+		uint64_t maxlen = jbd_get32(&jbd_fs->sb, maxlen);
+		if (maxlen == 0 ||
+		    maxlen > inode_size / ext4_sb_get_block_size(&fs->sb) ||
+		    jbd_get32(&jbd_fs->sb, first) >= maxlen ||
+		    jbd_get32(&jbd_fs->sb, start) >= maxlen) {
+			rc = EIO;
+			goto Error;
+		}
+	}
+
 	if (rc == EOK)
 		jbd_fs->bdev = fs->bdev;
 
